Keep a cooperative guest away from Windows-owned physical hardware.

The first free-running boot completed and returned from its ioctl, but froze
Windows immediately afterwards.  Its console log gives the mechanism rather
than merely a symptom: native PCI probing enumerated the host's real Ivy Bridge
chipset, NVIDIA GPU and Intel SATA controller, then ata_piix bound to
00:1f.2 and programmed it.  Cooperative Linux shares the processor; it does
not virtualize the physical I/O-port or MMIO namespaces, so that controller was
the one Windows was actively using.

This is the isolation boundary used by the original coLinux port, adapted to a
runtime-detected x86-64 guest:

  * physical port reads report an absent device and writes are discarded;
  * string port I/O is covered too, not just inb/outb;
  * ioremap refuses physical MMIO;
  * native PCI initialization never probes the host bus; and
  * PAT remains at the host's value, because IA32_PAT is per CPU rather than
    per address space and changing it changes the meaning of Windows' PTEs.

Synthetic coLinux devices use passage-page operations and do not need any of
these native paths.

--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/io.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/io.h
@@ -44,6 +44,52 @@
 #include <asm/shared/io.h>
